INT. SPIRAL STAIRWELL INSIDE A GIANT TORTOISE SHELL - NIGHT

The stairs wind through bone the colour of old ivory. Lanterns of green fire hang from hooks. Somewhere above, a vast slow heartbeat. Every third beat, the walls tremble.

ISOLDE VARN, 30s, in a ranger's grey cloak, climbs with one hand on the rail. A silver knife at her belt. She does not look back.

BRANWEN TOLL, 60s, the lamp-keeper, follows, sleeves smelling of smoke. She carries the only key.

CADOC, 20s, a boy with ink on his fingers and a satchel too big for him, stops to touch the wall. The bone warms under his palm.

PIP MARROW, 40s, a tinker with a hundred small bells sewn into his coat, brings up the rear. Each step jingles. He keeps trying to quiet them.

BRANWEN
Pip. The bells.

PIP
I'm stuffing them, Mother Toll.

BRANWEN
Stuff them harder.

The heartbeat THUDS. Dust sifts from the ceiling. Everyone stops. Isolde's hand drifts to her knife.

CADOC
Isolde, it knows we're here.

ISOLDE
It always knew.

She climbs. After a moment the others follow.

CADOC
You said it was asleep.

ISOLDE
I said it was old.

She does not meet his eyes. She taps the rail twice, a habit, a prayer.

BRANWEN
(to the stair, under her breath)
Steady, old girl. Steady.

PIP
Who's she talking to?

CADOC
The shell.

PIP
Does it answer?

CADOC
Sometimes.

They reach a landing. A door of black wood, banded in iron. Isolde pauses. She holds out a hand without looking. Branwen puts the key in it.

The key does not turn.

ISOLDE
Cadoc.

CADOC
Yes?

ISOLDE
Your hands.

He steps up. Presses his inky palm against the lock. The iron sighs. Bolts slide.

BRANWEN
Everyone, stay behind the line.

Pip shuffles back. A single bell chimes, high and clear.

The heartbeat stops.

Nobody breathes. A long moment. The lanterns gutter.

Then, from beyond the door, a sound like a very large animal clearing its throat. Gentle. Almost polite.

ISOLDE
(to the door)
We've come to ask a favour.

The door swings open on warm dark. The scent of rain and old coins pours out.

Isolde lowers her knife. Branwen lets out a breath she has held for forty years.

CADOC
Isolde?

ISOLDE
Go on.

He goes first.
